# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bayit environment variables. Copy to .env and fill in (for development; a staging or
# production site sets its variables in the hosting's settings, never from a copied .env).
# Required in every environment: SITE_ENV, DATABASE_URL, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, BETTER_AUTH_URL,
# APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY, HASH_SALT, NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL. The server REFUSES TO START when one is
# missing or unsafe (src/lib/startup-check.ts lists each rule; README → "Environment variables").
# Everything else is optional in development (a safe fallback is used).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which kind of site this is (src/lib/site-env.ts) — never decided by NODE_ENV:
#   "development"  your computer or the codespace (fake data; test mode and demo data allowed)
#   "staging"      the password-protected test site (test mode allowed behind its password)
#   "production"   the real site: no test mode (the server refuses to start with it), no demo
#                  logins, demo accounts or demo listings; only this one is indexed by Google
# Required: the server refuses to start when it is missing or misspelled.
SITE_ENV="development"

# Postgres connection string (Supabase, Neon, or local)
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://bayit:bayit@localhost:5432/bayit"

# Public address of the site, no trailing slash
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# The site's PERMANENT public address (https, the real domain, no trailing slash), e.g.
# "https://bayit.ge". Every link that leaves the site is built from it (src/lib/site-url.ts):
# calendar export links hosts paste into Airbnb / Booking.com, links in emails and text messages,
# share links, the sitemap, canonical and hreflang URLs (empty: those links use
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL).
# REQUIRED FOR GOOGLE INDEXING: only requests to SITE_URL's exact host count as the real site.
# While it is empty, or another host (bayit.ge against www.bayit.ge), every page says
# "noindex, nofollow" and robots.txt names no sitemap: Google indexes nothing. Use the host
# visitors actually open. Behind your own reverse proxy, pass the original Host (or
# X-Forwarded-Host) header through.
# While it is not a permanent https domain (localhost, a Codespaces / *.vercel.app preview or a
# tunnel), hosts see "the link will work once the site is on its permanent address" and can't copy
# their calendar link, and a production server prints a loud warning at startup.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL stays the address the site RUNS on, so a codespace can set SITE_URL to the real
# domain and still log in on its own address.
SITE_URL=""

# Better Auth — generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Encrypts ID documents and 2-step secrets — generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""
# Salt for hashing IPs/devices in logs — generate with: openssl rand -hex 16
HASH_SALT=""

# ── Login (src/lib/auth) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Order on the login sheet/page: Google (when set up) → a code by EMAIL (always; sent with Resend,
# see RESEND_API_KEY below) → a code by PHONE only in test mode (for the demo accounts). On the
# real site SMS codes are only used to verify a number once (a guest's booking, a host before
# posting). Codes live 10 minutes, die after 5 wrong tries; the server makes people wait 60, 120,
# 300, then 900 s between codes, with daily caps (phone 5, email 10, IP 30, device 10).
#
# Test mode: show login codes ON SCREEN (no SMS or email service needed; the demo accounts can log
# in). Only when SITE_ENV is "development" or "staging" (src/lib/site-env.ts), and then only for
# the site's own test address: localhost / 127.0.0.1 / a Codespaces preview on a development site,
# the site's own address on staging. With SITE_ENV="production" the server REFUSES TO START while
# it is "true". `npm run dev` shows no codes without it either. The server log warns while it is on.
AUTH_TEST_MODE="true"

# Google login — https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials (OAuth client, type "Web").
# Authorized redirect URI: <BETTER_AUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/google
# The "Continue with Google" button only appears when both are set.
# Use SEPARATE OAuth clients (SECURITY_REPORT 3.3.b, 12.2): one for the real site whose only
# redirect URI is the real domain's, and another for development / staging (localhost, the
# codespace, the staging address). Never add a localhost URI to the production client. Only verified
# Google emails can sign in, and Google never joins an existing email account by itself (the
# person connects it in Settings after a fresh code).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Apple login — https://developer.apple.com/account/resources (a Services ID + a Sign in with Apple key).
# Return URL: <BETTER_AUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/apple (Apple needs a real https domain).
# APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET is a JWT signed with your Apple key (valid for up to 6 months — renew it).
# APPLE_APP_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER is only needed for a native iOS app.
# The "Continue with Apple" button only appears when ID and secret are set.
APPLE_CLIENT_ID=""
APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
APPLE_APP_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER=""

# Phone codes (booking and host phone checks; phone login in test mode) and booking texts go
# through Twilio Programmable Messaging (founder's answer Q9; Twilio Verify is not used): the site
# makes the 6-digit code, stores only its keyed hash, and sends its own text ("never share this
# code") — just the SMS fee. SMS go ONLY to mobile numbers in Israel (+972) and Georgia (+995);
# at most 300 code texts a day (src/lib/auth/sms-guard.ts). Without these keys: test mode shows the
# code on screen; the real site says phone checks are "not available yet" and sends nothing.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=""
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=""
# The Messaging Service ("MG…", recommended: Console → Messaging → Services; its geo permissions
# must allow only Israel and Georgia) — or a sender number / alphanumeric sender ID.
TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=""
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=""

# Development only: let the DEMO staff accounts open /admin without 2-step login, on a development
# server (`npm run dev`) of a development site (SITE_ENV="development"). Never applies to a real
# account (the admin always needs Google Authenticator); a staging or production site refuses to
# start with it on.
DEV_SKIP_STAFF_2FA="false"

# ── Admin account (PRIVATE: never commit a real value) ───────
# The site account that becomes the admin (`npm run admin:grant -- --from-env`, also run by the
# codespace start): its email, else its phone in international format (+972… / +995…). The
# account must exist first (sign in once). In a codespace, set them as Codespaces secrets of the
# same names (github.com → Settings → Codespaces → Secrets): they are read from the environment
# and never written into this file. Empty = nobody is made admin. The admin alert address is
# ADMIN_ALERT_EMAIL (below).
BAYIT_ADMIN_EMAIL=""
BAYIT_ADMIN_PHONE=""

# ── Email (src/lib/email) ────────────────────────────────────
# Resend API key — https://resend.com/api-keys. Without it, emails are printed to the server log
# and kept in the admin "email outbox" (status LOGGED) instead of being sent. Email LOGIN codes
# need it on the real site (development / test mode show the code on screen instead). Login codes
# are masked (••••••) in the outbox.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
# Sender, on a domain verified in Resend, e.g. "Bayit <no-reply@bayit.example>".
# Defaults to Resend's test sender (onboarding@resend.dev), which only delivers to your own address.
EMAIL_FROM=""
# Booking alerts (src/lib/alerts): each host chooses email, Telegram and/or browser notifications
# in Settings → Booking alerts. An instant booking stands only once a channel that CONFIRMS
# delivery accepted the "new booking" alert: email (Resend returned an id) or Telegram (the bot's
# message went through). Browser notifications never count on their own. A host with no working
# confirming channel gets "Ask the host" instead of instant booking (development / test mode count
# the email outbox as delivered).
# Bounces and spam complaints: Resend → Webhooks → add endpoint <SITE_URL>/api/webhooks/resend with
# the events email.bounced, email.complained and email.delivered, and put its signing secret
# ("whsec_…") here. A bounce tells the founder and the guest right away and pauses only that host's
# email channel, until an email to them is delivered again or they change their address.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
# PRIVATE (never commit a real value): where admin alerts and the daily summary go
# (comma-separated; a different address from the admin account). In a codespace: a Codespaces
# secret of the same name (read from the environment, never copied here). Empty = the email
# addresses of the real (non-demo) admin accounts; with neither, alerts stay in the email outbox.
ADMIN_ALERT_EMAIL=""

# ── Telegram booking alerts (src/lib/alerts/telegram.ts; a free bot made with @BotFather) ──
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN: the token BotFather gives. TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME: the bot's username without
# "@" (the settings page links to t.me/<username>). TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET: any long random string
# (openssl rand -hex 32). Then register the webhook once (open in a browser):
#   https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/setWebhook?url=<SITE_URL>/api/webhooks/telegram&secret_token=<SECRET>
# Set them as Codespaces secrets of the same names (read from the environment) and on the hosting
# provider. Without the token and username, Telegram shows as "coming soon".
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=""
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=""
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# ── Browser notifications for booking alerts (src/lib/alerts/push.ts, web-push) ──
# A free VAPID key pair. scripts/ensure-vapid.mjs makes one in .env (the codespace runs it); make
# your own with `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`. Keep the SAME pair for good on each site:
# devices that turned notifications on stop getting them when it changes. The real site needs its
# OWN pair, generated fresh on the hosting (never the codespace's).
# VAPID_SUBJECT (optional): "mailto:…" or an https URL the push services can contact; default: the
# site address (https), else SUPPORT_EMAIL.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=""
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=""
VAPID_SUBJECT=""

# ── File storage (src/lib/storage) ───────────────────────────
# "local" (default): files under ./storage/public (served at /api/media/…) and ./storage/private.
# "s3": any S3-compatible storage — Cloudflare R2, Supabase Storage, AWS S3, MinIO.
STORAGE_DRIVER="local"
# Optional folder for the local driver (default: ./storage).
STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=""
# S3 endpoint, e.g. https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com or
# https://<project>.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3. Leave empty for AWS S3.
S3_ENDPOINT=""
# Region: "auto" for R2, your project region for Supabase, e.g. "eu-central-1" for AWS.
S3_REGION="auto"
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
# Bucket for listing photos (must be publicly readable).
S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET=""
# Bucket for ID and ownership documents (never public; files are also encrypted with APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY).
S3_PRIVATE_BUCKET=""
# Public base URL of the public bucket, no trailing slash, e.g. https://media.bayit.example or
# https://<project>.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/<bucket>
S3_PUBLIC_URL=""
# "true"/"false". Defaults to true when S3_ENDPOINT is set (needed by R2, Supabase and MinIO).
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=""

# ── Backups (Admin → Maintenance, npm run db:backup; src/lib/admin/maintenance) ──
# Folder for full database backups (personal data: never commit it). Empty = ./backups
# (git-ignored). A host without a lasting disk (Vercel) cannot keep them: use the database
# host's own backups there.
BACKUP_DIR=""

# ── CAPTCHA (src/lib/turnstile) ──────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare Turnstile — https://dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile → Add widget (mode "Managed").
# Without these, CAPTCHA checks are skipped (forms send the token "dev").
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=""
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=""

# ── Visitor IP (src/lib/request) ─────────────────────────────
# Header that holds the real visitor IP behind a proxy you trust, e.g. "cf-connecting-ip" behind
# Cloudflare. On Vercel leave it empty (Vercel's own headers are used). Used for rate limits and logs.
CLIENT_IP_HEADER=""

# ── Content-Security-Policy (src/lib/security/csp.ts) ────────
# Empty (the default): the stricter policy (images and media only from us, the photo bucket and
# the map tiles) runs as Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only; browsers report violations to
# /api/csp-report ("csp_report" security events). After a week without unexpected reports, set
# "enforce" to make it the enforced policy. Not a secret.
CSP_STRICT=""

# ── Listing photos & uploads (src/lib/images, src/lib/media) ─
# Public base URL that photo URLs are built from in the browser. Leave empty with the local
# driver ("/api/media/" is used). With STORAGE_DRIVER=s3 set it to the same value as S3_PUBLIC_URL
# (otherwise photos still work, through a redirect from /api/media/).
NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL=""
# Optional malware scanner for uploads (OFF when empty). Receives each file as a raw POST body and
# must answer JSON { "infected": boolean, "signature"?: string } — e.g. a small ClamAV wrapper.
# When set but unreachable, uploads fail with "file_scan_failed" (fail closed).
MALWARE_SCAN_URL=""
# Optional bearer token sent to MALWARE_SCAN_URL.
MALWARE_SCAN_TOKEN=""

# ── Listing translation (src/lib/translate) ──────────────────
# Titles and descriptions are translated into the other four languages when a listing is sent,
# approved or edited (cached in ListingTranslation). Without a provider key, translation is off:
# pages show the owner's original text with "Original in …" and a "Translate" link (Google
# Translate in the browser).
# Which service: "azure" (default when AZURE_TRANSLATOR_KEY is set; the founder's choice) or
# "google". Empty = azure if its key is set, else google if its key is set, else off.
TRANSLATE_PROVIDER=""
# Microsoft Azure AI Translator (Translator Text v3) — https://portal.azure.com → Create a resource
# → "Translator" (free tier F0: 2M characters a month). Keys and Endpoint → KEY 1 and the Location
# (region, e.g. "westeurope"; leave empty for a "global" resource).
AZURE_TRANSLATOR_KEY=""
AZURE_TRANSLATOR_REGION=""
# Google Cloud Translation v2 — https://console.cloud.google.com → APIs → "Cloud Translation API",
# then Credentials → API key restricted to that API (the second option).
GOOGLE_TRANSLATE_API_KEY=""

# ── Nearby places (src/lib/nearby) ───────────────────────────
# Supermarket and public-transport distances come from OpenStreetMap's Overpass API.
# "false" turns the lookup off (Jewish places and the airport still work). OVERPASS_URL can point
# to another Overpass instance (default https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter).
OVERPASS_ENABLED="true"
OVERPASS_URL=""

# ── Scam checks (src/lib/risk) ───────────────────────────────
# VoIP detection uses Twilio Lookup v2 (line type intelligence) with the same TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID /
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN as phone codes (≈ $0.008 per number, cached 90 days). Without Twilio the check
# is skipped. Set "false" to keep Twilio Messaging but skip Lookup.
TWILIO_LOOKUP_ENABLED="true"

# ── Scheduled jobs (src/app/api/cron, vercel.json) ───────────
# Shared secret for /api/cron/*. Vercel Cron sends "Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>" itself
# once this is set in the project. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32 (32+ characters: staging
# and production refuse to start without it).
# On a development server you can also run a job in the browser: /api/cron/expire-listings?secret=<CRON_SECRET>
CRON_SECRET=""

# ── Error monitoring (src/lib/monitoring.ts, src/instrumentation.ts) ──
# Sentry DSN (sentry.io → Project → Settings → Client Keys). Server crashes, unexpected errors in
# server actions and failed scheduled jobs are sent there — the path without its query string,
# never headers, cookies or user data. Empty: errors are only written to the server log.
SENTRY_DSN=""
# Optional labels; default to VERCEL_ENV / NODE_ENV and VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA.
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=""
SENTRY_RELEASE=""

# Extra hosts allowed to open the site, call Server Actions and log in (comma-separated hosts or
# origins), e.g. a tunnel address. GitHub Codespaces addresses and localhost:3000 are allowed
# automatically inside a codespace / in development (src/lib/auth/dev-origins.ts).
EXTRA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=""

# ── Map tiles (src/config/map.ts — the one place for the provider and key) ──
# CARTO basemaps: light = Voyager, dark = Dark Matter. Free for commercial use up to 1M tile
# requests a month. Get a key at https://carto.com/basemaps/apikey and, in the CARTO dashboard,
# restrict it to your production domain and *.app.github.dev. Without a key the maps still work
# (pins on a plain background) but show "Map key missing" in development / "map unavailable" in
# production — we never fall back to OpenStreetMap's own servers (their policy forbids apps).
# In a codespace, set it as a Codespaces secret named NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_KEY (read from the
# environment; the fast version rebuilds when it changes).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are built into the page: restart `npm run dev` / rebuild after changing them.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_KEY=""

# Address search in the post form (/api/geocode): OpenStreetMap's free Nominatim server by default,
# 1 request per second for the whole site, cached. Its User-Agent names NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL and
# SUPPORT_EMAIL. Optional: another Nominatim server (e.g. a self-hosted one).
# NOMINATIM_URL="https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search"
# Only "carto" is wired in so far; add another provider in src/config/map.ts to switch.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_PROVIDER="carto"
